  "findings": [
    "With equal priority the GLES eye copy queued behind the Vulkan decode: eye-copy CPU p90 was about 7.4-7.5 ms against well under 1 ms of copy work.",
    "At 120 Hz / 1000 Mbps / 4:2:0 a LOW global-priority decode queue cut eye-copy CPU p90 to about 1.8-1.9 ms in every pair, raised displayed target FPS (114.3 -> 117.7 on .32, 5/5 pairs; 110.6 -> 115.7 on .33, 4/5 pairs) and halved lost target frames (5.8 -> 2.8 and 9.6 -> 4.8 per s).",
    "LOW lengthens GPU decode (p90 about +1 ms) because decode is preempted; at 120 Hz there is headroom for that.",
    "At 144 Hz / 1000 Mbps / 4:2:0 decode is throughput-bound and LOW lowered displayed target FPS 135.0 -> 124.7 (3/3 pairs; lost 10.2 -> 20.2 per s), so it is not used there.",
    "Median estimated pipeline latency was lower with LOW in both 120 Hz sessions (56.3 -> 54.3 and 57.4 -> 52.2 ms), but the statistic is quantized and drifts between states; no latency claim is made."
  ],
  "decision": ".33 picks the decode queue priority at decoder creation: LOW for 4:2:0 at <=120 Hz, default otherwise; debug.q3pw.decode_priority=low|default overrides. Verified on .33 at 120 Hz.",
  "limitations": [
    "Stationary procedural chart on an unworn headset; not gameplay, perceptual or sustained thermal validation.",
    "Displayed target FPS counts frames that reached the client compositor; not optical display FPS.",
    "Estimated pipeline latency is ALVR's statistic, not measured motion-to-photon latency.",
    "Not measured: 4:4:4, bitrates other than 1000 Mbps, or refresh rates other than 120 and 144 Hz; the policy treats <=120 Hz 4:2:0 as having headroom by extrapolation.",
    "The 144 Hz session stopped after 7 of 10 blocks when one client relaunch did not reconnect; everything was restored."
  ]
